WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.940
Could one phished administrator
hand over our whole Microsoft 365?

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.900
Entra ID is where it breaks, in four ways.

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.360
First, too many people own the tenant.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.200
Global Administrator is the master key.

00:00:15.515 --> 00:00:18.705
It reads every mailbox and
changes every password.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:22.700
Most tenants give it permanently
to far more people than they think.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.730
Fool one of them,
and the whole tenant is taken.

00:00:26.783 --> 00:00:28.473
Second, the gate is open.

00:00:28.788 --> 00:00:33.438
Legacy sign-in sends a bare password
and cannot ask for a second factor.

00:00:33.673 --> 00:00:36.323
Some administrators are exempt from MFA,

00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:40.623
and personal phones nobody
manages open company mail.

00:00:40.908 --> 00:00:44.378
The company believes it has MFA,
but not where it counts.

00:00:45.483 --> 00:00:47.173
Third, the guests never left.

00:00:47.483 --> 00:00:49.773
Inviting an outsider takes one click.

00:00:50.048 --> 00:00:52.998
Removing one takes a decision nobody owns.

00:00:53.398 --> 00:00:56.808
Guests keep every Team and
folder they were ever added to.

00:00:57.028 --> 00:01:01.078
Board material stays readable by
people we no longer work with.

00:01:02.117 --> 00:01:04.427
Fourth, the back door between two worlds.

00:01:04.717 --> 00:01:07.727
A sync tool links our own
directory with the cloud.

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:11.557
Its server holds credentials strong
enough to change cloud accounts.

00:01:11.992 --> 00:01:15.142
So a breach in either world
becomes a breach of both.

00:01:16.217 --> 00:01:17.427
What good looks like.

00:01:17.712 --> 00:01:20.902
PIM means nobody is an
administrator by default.

00:01:21.282 --> 00:01:23.752
Aim for five eligible people or fewer.

00:01:24.762 --> 00:01:29.402
A Conditional Access baseline blocks
legacy sign-in and demands a second factor

00:01:29.402 --> 00:01:30.312
from everyone.

00:01:30.567 --> 00:01:33.357
Administrators get
passkeys or hardware keys.

00:01:33.717 --> 00:01:36.307
Every guest gets an owner and an expiry.

00:01:36.617 --> 00:01:38.967
The sync server is treated as tier zero.

00:01:39.272 --> 00:01:42.362
These are settings in a
licence we already pay for.

00:01:42.577 --> 00:01:46.747
NIS2 names access control and
secure sign-in among its measures.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:49.840
A suggested first step.

00:01:50.125 --> 00:01:53.995
Ask how many people can take over the
tenant today, and how many should.

00:01:54.305 --> 00:01:58.415
If the answer is above five,
or nobody knows, that is the finding.

00:02:00.017 --> 00:02:01.667
Four ways to lose the tenant.

00:02:02.017 --> 00:02:04.887
Fewer standing administrators,
and a closed gate.

